<section class="sec light">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="cards3">
      <article class="scn rv"><span class="scn__tag">Duplicate payment</span><h3>The same invoice, paid twice</h3><p>A supplier sends a reminder or a copy and both are processed.</p><dl><div><dt>How we find it</dt><dd>Comparing supplier, amount and date across all invoices, even when invoice numbers differ slightly.</dd></div><div><dt>What you do about it</dt><dd>Reclaim the money and add a control that catches this in future.</dd></div></dl></article>
      <article class="scn rv"><span class="scn__tag">Late payment</span><h3>Invoices paid after the due date</h3><p>The process is slow in one step, often approval.</p><dl><div><dt>How we find it</dt><dd>Lead time per step and per department or approver.</dd></div><div><dt>What you do about it</dt><dd>Tackle the bottleneck, for example with substitute rules for approvers.</dd></div></dl></article>
      <article class="scn rv"><span class="scn__tag">Skipped step</span><h3>Ordering after the invoice</h3><p>The purchase order is only created after the invoice has already arrived.</p><dl><div><dt>How we find it</dt><dd>Order of events per case compared with the designed process.</dd></div><div><dt>What you do about it</dt><dd>Tighten purchasing policy and discuss after-the-fact orders per department.</dd></div></dl></article>
      <article class="scn rv"><span class="scn__tag">Segregation of duties</span><h3>The same person creates and approves</h3><p>A basic control that is sometimes bypassed in practice.</p><dl><div><dt>How we find it</dt><dd>Link user to step and search for the combinations that should not occur.</dd></div><div><dt>What you do about it</dt><dd>Adjust rights in the system and investigate the exceptions.</dd></div></dl></article>
      <article class="scn rv"><span class="scn__tag">Rework</span><h3>Invoices that keep coming back</h3><p>An invoice is rejected, amended and submitted again.</p><dl><div><dt>How we find it</dt><dd>Loops in the process and the cause per supplier or category.</dd></div><div><dt>What you do about it</dt><dd>Fix the cause at the source, for example with better order information.</dd></div></dl></article>
      <article class="scn rv"><span class="scn__tag">Journal entries</span><h3>Corrections after the month-end close</h3><p>Entries that still change after closing, with consequences for reporting.</p><dl><div><dt>How we find it</dt><dd>Time and author of entries compared with the closing date.</dd></div><div><dt>What you do about it</dt><dd>Manage the closing calendar more tightly and remove structural causes.</dd></div></dl></article>
